# LilyPond PDF to MusicXML

A LilyPond PDF keeps every notehead, rest, flag and accidental as a font glyph at an exact
position, so nothing has to be recognised. On the Brumel test piece Halbestunde's scan was
unusable; the glyph read matched the print in all 62 bars of four voices after a bar-by-bar
proofread (two faults found, both fixed).

## 1. Check the PDF

```bash
pdfinfo score.pdf | grep Creator      # "LilyPond 2.x"
uv run --with pymupdf python -I -c "import pymupdf,sys; print({s['font'] for p in pymupdf.open(sys.argv[1]) for sp in p.get_texttrace() for s in [sp]})" score.pdf
```

Expect `Emmentaler-*` for the music and a text font (here `CenturySchL`). If the source `.ly`
exists, use `lilypond-to-score` instead. The scripts assume **four staves per system, one
voice per staff, no chords**: Cantus (treble) then three bass-clef voices. For other layouts
adapt `pitch_of` in `build.py` and the staff count in `extract.py`.

## 2. How the reading works

`pymupdf` `page.get_texttrace()` gives each glyph's id, origin and box. Staff lines are the
long horizontal drawn lines in groups of five. A glyph's **half-space index**
`hs = (bottom_line_y - y) / (space / 2)` gives the pitch: treble `hs 0` = E4, bass `hs 0` = G2.

Glyph ids at 15.9 pt (the notation size; check `glyph_dump`-style output for your file, ids are
per font subset but Emmentaler's low ids were stable here):

| gid | meaning | gid | meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | breve (8 beats) | 7 | flat |
| 3 | whole note | 8 | quarter rest |
| 4 | half note | 1 | half rest |
| 5 | black head | 0 | whole rest |
| 9 | dot | 6 | breve rest (8 beats) |
| 13 | up flag (head x + 5.3) | 16 | down flag (head x + 0.6) |
| 14, 15 | clefs | | |

- Stems are 0.2 wide rects; beams are filled quadrilaterals. Count beams on a stem (loosen the
  y tolerance for secondary beams) to get eighths and sixteenths.
- **Small 12.5 pt glyphs above the staff** at head x + about 1.6 are editorial ficta: gid 1 =
  natural, gid 2 = flat. They apply to that one note only. **gid 0 is the "L" editorial
  bracket, not an accidental** (reading it as a sharp put a wrong F♯ in the first run).
- **Mensurstriche**: barlines fall at multiples of 4 beats and long notes cross them. Build
  regular cut-time bars and tie across the barlines; do not use the barline x positions to
  assign bars.
- **Timing**: sum durations per staff for onsets. Each system's start is snapped to the
  barline residue mod 4. Invisible lead-in rests at a system start are anchored by columns
  shared with voices that start on the margin. `onset2.py` run on its own prints per-system totals
  and misaligned columns; **it must report 0 misaligned columns** before you go on.

## 3. Lyrics

The text layer is a permuted cipher: the extracted characters are not the printed letters, so
copying text gives nonsense. The real letters come from the glyph **shapes**. `lyr.py` carries
a gid-to-letter table (`SH`) for the Brumel PDF only, since glyph ids are per embedded font
subset. For a new PDF:

1. Pull the lyric font (`doc.extract_font(xref)`) and render its glyphs to a contact sheet
   with their ids (fontTools pens to SVG, then look at the picture).
2. Write the shape-to-letter string in gid order and replace `SH`.
3. Hyphens are **drawn rects** (about 2.3 wide, 0.22 high), not characters; extenders are long
   rects. `lyr.py` reads the hyphen rects; it does not reproduce extenders.

Syllables attach to the nearest note in order; a hyphen before or after sets `middle`/`end`.

## 4. Run it

```bash
S=/Users/wattd/dev/ai-score-editing-workflows/.claude/skills/lilypond-pdf-score/scripts
uv run --with pymupdf python -I $S/onset2.py score.pdf        # 0 misaligned in every system
uv run --with pymupdf python -I $S/build.py score.pdf         # overlaps/gaps per voice must be 0
uv run --with pymupdf python -I $S/mxl.py score.pdf out.musicxml \
  --title "Title" --composer "Name" --names Cantus,Contra,Tenor,Bassus --fifths -2
```

`mxl.py` writes divisions 4, cut time (2/2 `symbol="cut"`), ties split at barlines, shown
accidentals for printed flats, ficta and courtesy naturals, lyrics on the first tied piece.
The top voice is written as treble clef with octave-change -1 and sounding pitch one octave
below the printed note, which matches Sibelius "Treble down 8". Check the time signature and
clef against the print yourself.

## 5. Import and proofread (Sibelius)

Import with `OpenScore` (the "Open MusicXML File" dialog needs a click on OK, see the
`sibelius` skill), then `ExportScore ... SIBL`. Compare **every bar of every voice against the
print**, page by page; Sibelius re-lays the systems, but bar numbers and notes should match.
Fix in place with `apply`: `set-pitch` for a wrong note, `delete` (type LyricItem) plus
`add-lyric` for words (syllableType only `middle` or `end`; use `middle` where a hyphen follows).

Known gaps after a run: some hyphens in the voices you did not check word by word may be
missing; the print's lyric extender lines are not reproduced; system and page breaks differ
from the print.

## Verified on

Brumel, "Du tout plongiet - Fors seulement", 3 pages, two flats, cut-C: 62 bars, four voices,
all pitches, ties, accidentals, rests and lyrics checked against the print.
